Date: Wed, 09 Jun 93 21:06:01 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: PHYSICS: Question

Derek Zahn:

> The scenario:  Imagine a piece of the universe devoid of
> matter except for two hydrogen atoms, approaching each
> other.  Each has a single electron.  As they get nearer,
> the electrons' energy changes.

The atoms are electrically neutral so each atom only feels the effect
of the Van der Waals attraction (which falls off r^-6, I think).  Is
that what you mean?

> 1. How is this change in energy "transmitted"?  I presume
> that it is via photons, which are distinct entities.  These
> might be "virtual photons".

They are.  Virtual photons (like all virtual particles) are not
constrained by the classical energy-momentum equations.  They carry 
(and hence transmit) as little or as much energy and momentum as they
like.

> 2. What equation do I use to figure out the probability of
> a photon being transmitted between these atoms (causing
> the change in energy)?  I was told by one physicist that
> the probability actually is ZERO at some distance, but
> that doesn't seem right to me.  I'd guess that it simply
> falls off to nearly zero rather quickly.  Similarly, what
> formulae do I use to figure out the energy quanta that are
> transferred?

The formulae can be expressed compactly as functional path integrals,
but to get numbers out you have to expand as an inifinite series of
Feynman diagrams.  Each line in each diagram contributes a term to
integrate over.  And the series is strongly divergent (yes, you read
that right - one of the reasons why I wondered if Omega's series was
provably convergent, which no one answered - but I digress).  This is
typical of all interesting expansions in QED - they're all strongly
divergent.  No way is currently known of getting precise answers in
principle.  In practice we can approximate to large degrees of accuracy,
by ignoring the later, divergent terms by a trick called Borel
summation.  Physics gets into deep problems here.  [note: this is a
separate problem from renormalisation - another can of worms]

> 3. Again similarly, as they get closer and the orbitals deform
> much more, how many photon transfers take place, and what is
> their energy?  What theory do I apply to calculate this?

How many virtual photon transfers take place is often not a well defined
property.  Field strength and the corresponding virtual particle number
are non-commutating parameters, obeying uncertainty restrictions. 

However, real photon number emission (to infinity) is well defined.  The
system for two Van der Waals atoms has a lower energy when they are
together than when they are widely separated.  The energy deficit is
distributed amongst an _infinite_ number of soft, low frequency photons. 
[which answers the problem, often posed, of finding a physical infinity] 
This is known as the infra-red divergence, and occurs whenever a charge
is accelerated [and that's pretty often :-)].  It's like Zeno's paradox. 
The number of photons with wavelength longer than any finite amount is
always infinite, although the energy associated with them all is finite.

> derek

