Date: Wed, 9 Jun 93 14:39:38 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: QM: Theories vs. Interpretations

Tim May gives the standard physics party line on QM - those differences of
opinion are "interpretation" and "philosophy", and students should not
worry their pretty little heads about it.  And much of the pedagodgy of
teaching QM consists of rituals to encourage students to stop asking why
and get on with learning how to do useful stuff.

This is similar to the way Minsky speculates that jokes are all about what
things we shouldn't think about - we laugh and suddenly don't feel like
pursuing that line of thought.  Both of these are functional adaptations,
in usual practice.

But let's not forget they are just rhetorical/mental tricks.  Similar
tricks are used by most people to ignore thinking about the distant future,
the disfunctionality afflicting many of us extropians, or to imagine
political structures radically different from our current ones.

Anyway, there is no hard distinction between "theories" and
"interpretations", or between "science" and "philosophy".  Sometimes two
different formalisms seem to imply exactly the same observations.
Sometimes this can be proved exactly, and we end up thinking of them as
just two different ways of saying the same thing.  Like say rectangular vs.
polar coordinates describing a 2D point.  Sometimes they just seem the same
for a while, but eventually turn out to be different, implying different
observations and other consequences.

It is certainly *not* known, as Tim claims, that "These [QM]
interpretations typically have no testable consequences".  No one has yet
been able to devise a currently doable test, but that doesn't imply no one
will.  And people have been able to imagine tests, not yet doable.

I like the many-worlds interpretation because it at least seems to make
clear predictions about the hard case of the measurement of a observer,
where it's not clear that other interpretations can even represent this
process, much less predict it.  

I talked with John Bell for about ~1/2 hr ~ a year before he died.  Bell
favored an interpretation whereby states projected stochastically when they
became spatially extended beyond a certain point.  Penrose favors a similar
theory.  This approach does make predictions about measuring of observers,
and clearly different predictions than many worlds, though its not clear
how easy it would be to observe.  Even this approach must allow superposed
observer states, as in many worlds, if we can construct spatially compact
observers.

I think Deutch proposed a test whereby an observer running on a quantum
computer is split into a superposition of observers doing different things,
but who are at the end just asked a one bit question: "how's things, still
feel real, anything funny?".  If they all answer the same, the
superposition may be rejoined, and we can verify that by measurement.  This
verifies the superposition of observers.

This need not rule out the theory Bell favored, but it would demonstrate
that observer superposition explains some measurement processes.  Many
worlds simply says that this is the only measurement process.  Measurements
for us might still be either just superposition, or some extra stochastic
process.

Looks like I've typed too much already, so I'll stop here.  

Robin Hanson

