Date: Wed, 9 Jun 93 15:20:40 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Omega Convergence, Chaitin, and Rudy Rucker

I plan to be very brief here, as I have no intention of writing more
essays explaining the Chaitin results. But Mike Price has made a
claim:


> integrate over.  And the series is strongly divergent (yes, you read
> that right - one of the reasons why I wondered if Omega's series was
> provably convergent, which no one answered - but I digress).  This is

This was answered several times by at least two of us.

First, there is no _single_ Omega, as the exact form of the Turing
Machine picked will determine the digits of Omega-sub-that-machine.
Thus, a specific Macintosh IIci running Think C 5.0 (and with other
set constraints, such as memory, etc.), might produce an apprimation
to Omega of 0.987.... Another configuration might produce 0.9991...

(Omega is typically close to 1 for modern computers because nearly all
randomly selected programs halt the machine. It's the digits further
out that are more "random.")

So, the point is not that there is some exact Omega, like pi or e,
that can someday be calculated. It is best seen as a concept
expressing the maximal compression of the halting problem.

Second, as has been mentioned many times, it gets harder and harder to
compute successive digits of Omega. Much harder, as the digits are
encoding the answers to tough problems.

I would not call this a "divergent" series, but is surely is not a
convergent series in the sense of a computation homing in on the final
answer, getting closer and closer.

(Does the number Omega even exist? Given that it is the probability a
class of random programs halts a particular machine (equivalently,
that a particular program halts a class of random Turing machines),
and this is between 0 and 1 inclusive, it exists by definition.
Whether it can be calculated is another matter: the halting problem,
for a given program. But it exists, and can be approximated, at least
by experimental methods, evolution, genetic programming, etc., which
is Chaitin's main point about a new era of experimental mathematics.)

Each digit of Omega takes exponentially (or worse) more time
to compute than the previous one. But the values remain bounded in the
interval  [0, 1], so I wouldn't call it "divergent." 

I hope this answers Mike's question.

By the way, I mentioned to Rudy Rucker the interest in Chaitin's work
on this list (I was hinting to him that he ought to try subscribing!)
and that several folks were reading "Mind Tools." He says that after
he finishes an artificial life program he's been working on, he may
write another book on computers.

-Tim May

