From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: QM: Theories vs. Interpretations

Robin Hanson writes:

> Tim May gives the standard physics party line on QM - those differences of
> opinion are "interpretation" and "philosophy", and students should not
> worry their pretty little heads about it.  And much of the pedagodgy of
> teaching QM consists of rituals to encourage students to stop asking why
> and get on with learning how to do useful stuff.
....

> It is certainly *not* known, as Tim claims, that "These [QM]
> interpretations typically have no testable consequences".  No one has yet
> been able to devise a currently doable test, but that doesn't imply no one
> will.  And people have been able to imagine tests, not yet doable.

This is what I said. These different interpretations make no
different predictions, nothing that is falsifiable in a Popperian
sense. The advocates of the Foo Interpretation have not said "Our
interpretation predicts that Experiment X will produce Result Y. If it
does not, we stand ready to abandon our interpretation."

No one has devised any test of the major interpretations. Could they
in the future? Sure. But they haven't yet.

(John Kramer believes he may able to to test his "transactional"
theory, but this is not yet one of the major interpretations.)

Robin goes on to mention other work. Bear in mind I never said such
work on the foundations of QM is pointless, only that existing
interpretations have not yet produced falsifiable tests. I did mention
the Aharonov experiments, which to my mind are the most important
tests now being proposed. Should Aharonov find a way to measure psi,
instead of psi-squared, then perhaps the interpretations of QM can be
tested.

Until then, and since I'm not a quantum philosopher, I'm not going to
"worry my pretty little head" about it. But over a few beers, I'll be
happy to BS with anybody about it!

My main point was directed at the advocates of the "holographic
model." If it just restates the Standard Model and adds some newage
about everything being part of the Cosmic All, with no predictions and
no testable consequences ("yet"), what's the point?

-Tim May

