Ray says:
> Edward J OConnell writes:
> > Girls learn before boys that being a geek is very, very uncool.
> 
>   And this I think is the real cause. Not some feminist patriachal
> conspiracy for keeping girls out of science but a general negative
> stereotype against sciencetists in general.  The difference may be
> that a boy's interest to sciences are stronger and his testosterone
> agressiveness allows him to overcome the peer pressure better
> than girls.  When our culture values the football quarterback and the
> beauty queen over the scientist and mathematician, it's not suprising
> we see a disparity between the sexes and classes.

Indeed, I have noticed that women motivated to do work in computers or
mathematics are generally as good as the average guy in the fields.
The women in the field at all, however, are so few and far between
that one is lead to wonder about why. I've screened dozens of
applicants for system administration work here at Lehman -- and in
many batches of several dozen never even had a single female
applicant! Those women who we do have here, however (not that many
given that so few apply) seem to follow largely the same distribution
of skill as the men -- but since there are only a few "superhackers"
here, its to be expected that statistically none of the handful of
women would fall into the category -- after all, any random handful
of the men are unlikely to fall into it, either!

However, my anecdotal evidence isn't really a scientific survey.
Unfortunately, I distrust most sociologists, who seem to follow
cargo-cult scientific methodologies rather than rigorous ones. Perhaps
someday we will know the real answer to such questions.

Perry

